  {
   "user": "Sound the active buzzer for half a second every few seconds, from GPIO18.",
   "assistant": "Now the program: 0.5 s on, 3 s off.I built it: the active buzzer beeps for 0.5 s, then stays quiet for 3 s, over and over. GPIO18 switches it through a transistor. ERC shows 0 errors and 0 warnings, and the simulation gives the numbers below.\n\n**What I built**\n- **Q1 (PN2222 transistor):** its three legs are in rows 40, 41 and 42, column b.\n  - Row 40 (emitter) goes to GND.\n  - Row 41 (base) goes through R1 to GPIO18.\n  - Row 42 (collector) has an orange lead to the buzzer's − leg.\n- **R1 (1 kΩ resistor):** goes from 41d across the centre gap to 41f. GPIO18 connects to 41j.\n- **BZ1 (buzzer):** + is in 53e and connects to 5V. − is in 50e.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Why the transistor:** the buzzer is a 5 V part and draws about 30 mA. That's more than a GPIO pin should supply (16 mA max), and the pins only give 3.3 V. So GPIO18 just turns the transistor on, and the transistor connects the buzzer to ground, powered from the 5 V pin.\n- **Why 1 kΩ:** (3.3 V − 0.75 V) ÷ 1 kΩ ≈ 2.5 mA into the transistor's base. The sim shows 2.45 mA from GPIO18. That's well under the pin's limit and plenty to switch the buzzer fully on.\n- **Sim results:**\n  - **GPIO18 high:** the buzzer sounds with 4.93 V across it. The transistor drops only 0.07 V, and the 5V supply draws 29.6 mA.\n  - **GPIO18 low:** the buzzer draws 0 mA.\n- **The program:** it uses gpiozero's `Buzzer(18)`. Run in the sim, it switched GPIO18 high at 0.0, 3.5 and 7.0 s, each time for 0.5 s. Ctrl+C turns the buzzer off cleanly. To change \"every few seconds\", edit `OFF_TIME`.\n\n**What to check on the bench**\n- **Transistor direction:** hold it with the flat, printed face toward you. The legs are then emitter, base, collector from left to right. Emitter goes in row 40, collector in row 42. If it's backwards, the buzzer will stay silent or be very faint.\n- **Buzzer direction:** the long leg (+, marked on top) goes in row 53, and the short leg goes in row 50. Peel off the sticker if it has one.\n- **Active vs passive buzzer:** the kit has both. The active one has a sealed black underside. The passive one shows a green circuit board underneath and only clicks with this code.\n- **Ribbon cable:** the brown wire of the ribbon marks pin 1 (3V3). It must line up with the T-board's pin 1 at row 1.\n\nI couldn't take a picture of the layout because the 3D view wasn't available in this session. I left a few empty rows between the transistor and the buzzer so the buzzer's 12 mm body doesn't press against the transistor.",
